Here is a quick comparison of mutating the original versus spreading into a new copy.

```javascript
const original = [1, 2, 3];

// Mutation: changes 'original'
const mutated = original;
mutated.push(4);
console.log('Mutated:', original); // [1, 2, 3, 4]

// Copying: keeps 'original' intact
const copy = [...original];
copy.push(5);
console.log('Copy:', original); // [1, 2, 3, 4]
console.log('New:', copy); // [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
```

Here is a quick comparison. Mutation changes the original array, while copying creates a new one so you don't accidentally break other parts of your code.

```javascript
const original = [1, 2, 3];

// Mutation: modifies 'original' directly
const mutated = original;
mutated.push(4);
console.log(original); // [1, 2, 3, 4]

// Copying: creates a new reference using spread syntax
const copied = [...original];
copied.push(5);
console.log(original); // [1, 2, 3, 4] (still modified from previous step)
console.log(copied); // [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
```

